QUESTIONS=[
'Find general solutions: (a) (y′)²=1−y²; (b) e^x y′+y²=0; (c) √(1−x²) dy=√(1−y²) dx; (d) cosec²x dy+sec y dx=0.',
'Reduce to separable form and solve: (a) y′=(y+x)²; (b) y′=tan(x+y)−1; (c) y′=y/x+y²/x²; (d) y′=y/x+x²/y².',
'Solve homogeneous equations: (a) y′=(x+y)/(x−y); (b) y′=(xy−y²)/x²; (c) y′=(x²+3y²)/(2xy); (d) y′=(xy+y²)/(x²+xy+y²).',
'Reduce to homogeneous form and solve: (a) xy′=y+√(x²+y²), y(4)=3; (b) (x⁴+y⁴) dx=2x³y dy, y(1)=0.',
'The slope at P(x,y) is (y−1)/(1−x). Find the curve through P(4,−3).',
'Find the solution curve of xyy′=3y²+x² through P(−1,2).',
'Find the real portion of the solution curve of x e^(y/x) dx+y dx=x dy through P(1,0).',
'A particle moves along the x-axis with velocity equal to half its abscissa minus three times the time. At t=2,x=−4. Determine its motion along the x-axis.',
'Oil consumption rate (billions of barrels) is dx/dt=1.2e^(0.04t), with t=0 corresponding to 1990. Find oil used from 1990 to 1995 and over t=8 years.',
'Infection rate (people per month) is dI/dt=100t/(t²+1), with t months since outbreak. Find total infected people over the first four months.',
'Determine orthogonal trajectories: (a) y=cx³; (b) xy=c; (c) y=cxe^x; (d) y²=x²+C. [The source repeats question number 10.]'
]
STEPS=[
 ['(a) On a branch with |y|<1, choose y′=±√(1−y²). Integration gives arcsin y=±x+C locally, hence sinusoidal arcs with derivative sign consistent with the chosen branch.','The squared equation also has constant solutions y=1 and y=−1. Smooth solutions may join sinusoidal arcs to waits at these endpoints, so a single sine family does not exhaust every solution.','(b) y′=−e^(−x)y². Retain y=0; for y≠0 integrate dy/y²=−e^(−x)dx to obtain −1/y=e^(−x)+C. Thus y=1/(A−e^(−x)), excluding denominator zeros.','(c) On |x|<1, |y|<1, separate dy/√(1−y²)=dx/√(1−x²). Hence arcsin y=arcsin x+C, with both principal ranges respected.','The source radicals are real only for |x|≤1 and |y|≤1. Also retain constant y=±1, which division removes. The unsquared equation requires nonnegative y′ in the interior; unrestricted sine branches would add false solutions.','(d) The original equation requires sin x≠0 and cos y≠0. Multiply the slope equation by cos y: cos y dy=−sin²x dx.','Integrate: sin y=−x/2+sin(2x)/4+C. Choose implicit branches with cos y≠0 and intervals excluding sin x=0; differentiating recovers the original equation.'],
 ['(a) Set u=x+y, so u′=1+y′=1+u². Separate to get arctan u=x+C, hence y=tan(x+C)−x away from tangent poles.','(b) Set u=x+y: u′=tan u. For sin u≠0, separate cot u du=dx and integrate ln|sin u|=x+C.','Thus sin(x+y)=Ae^x locally with cos(x+y)≠0. Include A=0, giving u=nπ and y=nπ−x, the equilibrium-u solutions discarded by division.','(c) Set u=y/x, x≠0. Then u+xu′=u+u², so xu′=u². For u≠0, integrate −1/u=ln|x|+C.','Hence y=−x/(ln|x|+C), with no denominator zero; also include y=0.','(d) With u=y/x and x,y≠0, xu′=1/u². Integrate u²du=dx/x to get u³=3ln|x|+C.','Thus y=x·cuberoot(3ln|x|+C) on intervals where this cube-root factor is nonzero. Differentiation checks all four equations and their original denominator restrictions.'],
 ['(a) Put u=y/x, x≠0: xu′=(1+u²)/(1−u). Integrate (1−u)du/(1+u²)=dx/x.','This gives arctan u−(1/2)ln(1+u²)=ln|x|+C, or arctan(y/x)−ln√(x²+y²)=C on a continuous local angle chart. Retain x−y≠0.','(b) Put u=y/x: xu′=−u². For u≠0 integrate 1/u=ln|x|+C, hence y=x/(ln|x|+C). Also include y=0; original x≠0 remains.','(c) Put u=y/x with x,y≠0: xu′=(1+u²)/(2u). Integrate 2u du/(1+u²)=dx/x.','Thus ln(1+u²)=ln|x|+C, giving x²+y²=A|x|³, A>0, on intervals with y≠0. There are no real constant-u roots since 1+u²>0.','(d) Put u=y/x: xu′=−u³/(1+u+u²). For u≠0 integrate −(u^(−3)+u^(−2)+u^(−1))du=dx/x.','Hence 1/(2u²)+1/u−ln|u|=ln|x|+C, or x²/(2y²)+x/y−ln|y|=C. Also retain y=0 for x≠0.','The denominator x²+xy+y² vanishes only at the origin. The last implicit expression can extend across x=0,y≠0; direct differentiation verifies the original equation there as well.'],
 ['(a) Near x=4>0 put u=y/x. Then xu′=√(1+u²). Integrate asinh u=ln x+C, equivalently u+√(1+u²)=Ax with A>0.','At x=4,y=3, u=3/4 and u+√(1+u²)=2, so A=1/2. Thus y+√(x²+y²)=x²/2.','Solve the relation: y=x²/4−1. Check √(x²+y²)=x²/4+1, so xy′=x²/2=y+√(x²+y²), and y(4)=3. The polynomial solution satisfies the source equation even at x=0.','(b) A finite derivative at (1,0) would require 1+0=2·1³·0·y′=0, a contradiction. Therefore no classical differentiable y(x) solution satisfies this IVP.','A geometric integral curve can still have a vertical tangent there. Put z=y²: x⁴+z²=x³z′. Then u=z/x² gives xu′=(u−1)².','For u≠1 integrate −1/(u−1)=ln|x|+C. Using u(1)=0 gives C=1. Thus y²=x² ln x/(1+ln x).','For x just above 1, the geometric branches are y=±x√(ln x/(1+ln x)); their derivatives diverge at x=1. They satisfy the differential form as a curve, but do not provide a differentiable y(x) IVP at that endpoint.','The divided-out u=1 curves y=±x solve the differential form but fail y(1)=0. Keep the distinction between a geometric integral curve and a classical function solution.'],
 ['Write y′=(y−1)/(1−x), retaining x≠1.','The constant y=1 is a solution, but does not pass through (4,−3).','For y≠1, separate dy/(y−1)=dx/(1−x).','Integrate ln|y−1|=−ln|1−x|+C, hence (y−1)(1−x)=K.','At (4,−3), K=(−4)(−3)=12. Thus y=1+12/(1−x).','The interval containing x=4 is x>1. Differentiate y′=12/(1−x)², equal to (y−1)/(1−x); y(4)=−3.'],
 ['Use z=y², giving z′=2yy′. The equation becomes z′−6z/x=2x with x≠0.','Multiply by integrating factor x^(−6): (z x^(−6))′=2x^(−5).','Integrate: z x^(−6)=−(1/2)x^(−4)+C, hence y²=Cx⁶−x²/2.','Use x=−1,y=2: 4=C−1/2, so C=9/2.','Choose the positive branch through (−1,2): y=√((9x⁶−x²)/2). Its interval is x<−1/√3.','At the endpoint y=0, the original equation would demand 0=x²≠0, so the classical solution cannot include that endpoint. Differentiating y² verifies 2xyy′=27x⁶−x², or xyy′=3y²+x² after substitution.'],
 ['Divide the source form by x dx with x≠0: y′=e^(y/x)+y/x.','Set u=y/x, so y′=u+xu′ and xu′=e^u.','Separate e^(−u)du=dx/x and integrate −e^(−u)=ln|x|+C.','At (1,0), u=0 gives C=−1. Thus e^(−u)=1−ln x on the positive-x chart.','For a real solution require 1−ln x>0, so 0 < x < e. Taking logs gives y=−x ln(1−ln x).','Differentiate: y′=−ln(1−ln x)+1/(1−ln x)=y/x+e^(y/x). Also y(1)=0, confirming the entire real interval through the initial point.'],
 ['Translate the velocity statement into x′=x/2−3t.','Try a linear particular solution x=at+b: a=(at+b)/2−3t. Coefficients give a=6,b=12.','The homogeneous equation x′=x/2 has solution Ce^(t/2). Thus x(t)=6t+12+Ce^(t/2).','Use x(2)=−4: −4=24+Ce, so C=−28/e.','Therefore x(t)=6t+12−28e^((t−2)/2).','Differentiate: x′=6−14e^((t−2)/2)=x/2−3t, and x(2)=−4. The quantity is signed position, rather than cumulative distance.'],
 ['The total oil used during an interval is the integral of the specified consumption rate, in billions of barrels.','For T years since 1990, integrate ∫₀ᵀ1.2e^(0.04t)dt=[30e^(0.04t)]₀ᵀ.','Hence the amount is 30(e^(0.04T)−1). The subtraction removes the initial antiderivative constant.','From 1990 to 1995, T=5: amount=30(e^0.2−1)≈6.64208 billion barrels.','For t=8 years, amount=30(e^0.32−1)≈11.31383 billion barrels.','Differentiating the cumulative expression recovers 1.2e^(0.04T), and at T=0 the used amount is zero.'],
 ['The additional number infected over the first four months is the integral of the source rate from t=0 to t=4.','Set u=t²+1, so du=2t dt.','Integrate 100t/(t²+1): an antiderivative is 50ln(t²+1).','Evaluate [50ln(t²+1)]₀⁴=50ln17−50ln1=50ln17.','This is approximately 141.66067, about 142 people as a continuous-model estimate.','An initial infected count is not supplied: the integral gives new infections over the interval; a cumulative total including existing cases would additionally require I(0). Differentiation verifies the given rate.'],
 ['At regular intersections replace the family slope by its negative reciprocal, using tangent vectors for horizontal/vertical exceptions.','(a) From y=cx³, y′=3y/x. Orthogonal y′=−x/(3y) integrates to x²+3y²=K. Regular trajectories have K>0.','(b) From xy=c, y′=−y/x. Orthogonal y′=x/y integrates to y²−x²=K. Restrict to regular intersections of the original family.','(c) From y=cxe^x, y′=y(x+1)/x. Orthogonal y′=−x/[(x+1)y].','Integrate y dy=−[1−1/(x+1)]dx to obtain y²+2x−2ln|x+1|=K on x≠−1.','Also the vertical line x=−1 is an orthogonal trajectory, because every original-family tangent there is horizontal. The common origin of the original family is singular as a family and needs exclusion from regular orthogonality claims.','(d) From y²=x²+C, y′=x/y. Orthogonal y′=−y/x integrates to xy=K. For K=0, the coordinate axes give the horizontal/vertical exceptional trajectories away from singular intersections.','Differentiate each implicit result to recover its orthogonal slope. At slope exceptions test perpendicular tangent vectors rather than divide by zero.']
]
ANSWERS=['Local sinusoidal arcs plus y=±1; y=1/(A−e^(−x)) plus zero; arcsin y−arcsin x=C plus y=±1; sin y=−x/2+sin2x/4+C, with stated domains.','(a) tan(x+C)−x; (b) sin(x+y)=Ae^x; (c) −x/(ln|x|+C), plus zero; (d) x·cuberoot(3ln|x|+C), retaining denominators.','(a) arctan(y/x)−ln√(x²+y²)=C locally; (b) x/(ln|x|+C) plus zero; (c) x²+y²=A|x|³; (d) x²/(2y²)+x/y−ln|y|=C plus zero.','(a) y=x²/4−1; (b) no classical differentiable y(x) IVP; geometric curve y²=x² ln x/(1+ln x) has a vertical tangent at (1,0).','y=1+12/(1−x), x>1.','y=√((9x⁶−x²)/2), x<−1/√3.','y=−x ln(1−ln x), 0 < x < e.','x(t)=6t+12−28e^((t−2)/2).','About 6.64208 and 11.31383 billion barrels, respectively.','50ln17≈141.66067 additional infections, about 142.','(a) x²+3y²=K; (b) y²−x²=K; (c) y²+2x−2ln|x+1|=K plus x=−1; (d) xy=K, including axis exceptions.']
